{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 97 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "758e49721f006ef37d4d79e6a8875e06a084269788cd3bcd65f62796718a3109" :edb/db-before-t 96 :edb/db-after-t 97 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533409 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:45:12.516Z" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186054755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292731 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186054761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292705 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186054761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292711 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186054810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292722 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186054810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292723 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186054811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292724 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052394 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186055193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291860 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055193 1071 "For which values of $x$ is $x^{2} - 3x - 10 > 0$?" 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186055193 1071 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-3x-10>0?$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055193 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x-10 & >0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+2)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-2$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-2),$ $(-2,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+2)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+2)(x-5)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+2)(x-5)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-3x-10>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052406 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186055195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291803 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055195 1071 "For which values of $x$ is $3x^{2} - 3x - 36 \\le 0$?" 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186055195 1071 "For which values of $x$ is $3{x}^{2}-3x-36\\le 0?$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186055195 1074 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3x-36 & \\le 0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-x-12) & \\le 0 \\\\ 3(x+3)(x-4) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+3)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+3)(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $3(x+3)(x-4)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $3(x+3)(x-4)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-3,4).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(x+3)(x-4)\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [-3,4].$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292758 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292754 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292747 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292748 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059672 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292744 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059674 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292678 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292656 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292679 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292685 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292691 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292838 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292839 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292848 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059750 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292849 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059751 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292850 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292852 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292876 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292878 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292879 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292861 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292862 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059759 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292869 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292892 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059761 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292893 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059762 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292891 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292863 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059793 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292799 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292854 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292856 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292809 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292851 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186059853 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292874 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186060264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058176 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186060264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292119 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186060264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186060264 1071 "The point $(1, 4)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$. Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = f(x - 1) + 1$?" 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186060264 1071 "The point $(1,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x-1)+1?$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186060264 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292693 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064169 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292810 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292689 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292690 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292692 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064170 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292736 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292613 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292616 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064216 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292617 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292548 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062011 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186064664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291802 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064664 1071 "![](images/d4/d4c0faafff52f414c30a4da32f9f0ba3520ef88d7c8bebe5c12a7ccea3109d17.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The area of the puddle increases at a rate of $40\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the radius of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $5\\text{ cm}?$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064664 1071 "![](images/d4/d4c0faafff52f414c30a4da32f9f0ba3520ef88d7c8bebe5c12a7ccea3109d17.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The area of the puddle increases at a rate of $40πcm^{2}/min$. How fast is the radius of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $5cm$?" 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186064664 1074 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=5,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi $ and $r=5,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (5) \\\\ 40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 10\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is increasing at a rate of $4$ centimeters per minute." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062071 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186064674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291885 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064674 1071 "Two cars drive toward an intersection. The cars are traveling at right angles to each other, and each car's speed is $30\\,\\text{mph}.$ If car $A$ is $12$ miles from the intersection and car $B$ is $35$ miles from the intersection, how fast is the distance between the cars decreasing? Give your answer to one decimal place." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064674 1071 "Two cars drive toward an intersection. The cars are traveling at right angles to each other, and each car's speed is $30mph$. If car $A$ is $12$ miles from the intersection and car $B$ is $35$ miles from the intersection, how fast is the distance between the cars decreasing? Give your answer to one decimal place.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186064674 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/f9/f9396718785574a63b0e1b7f621a36fee01a4d555dc67e06cf19f17bf718ba7e.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car B and the intersection,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car A and the intersection, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $y=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $x=35\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {35}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 1\\,369 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =37\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}$ when $x=35\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=12,$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}$ when $x=35\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=12.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the intersection is decreasing for both cars. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(35)(-30)+2(12)(-30) & =2(37)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2\\,820 & =74\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-38.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is decreasing at a rate of $38.1\\,\\text{mph}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062101 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186064679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292509 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064679 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\tan x\\sqrt{\\cos x}dx$." 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186064679 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186064679 1074 "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068786 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292712 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292709 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292853 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068837 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292859 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292701 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292703 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068861 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292704 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292546 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292936 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292937 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068890 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292938 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292939 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292738 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292740 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292741 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186068906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292782 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066370 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186069203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292468 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069203 1071 "![](images/60/60a01bfb824bb2aae025aacd937726ac786b3c69842baf619de94570157df252.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y = (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} + c$. What is the value of $c$?" 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186069203 1071 "![](images/60/60a01bfb824bb2aae025aacd937726ac786b3c69842baf619de94570157df252.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ . What is the value of $c?$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069203 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5.$ Therefore, $c=-5.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066376 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186069204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292083 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069204 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y = (\\frac{1}{2})^{x} + 4$?" 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186069204 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+4?$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069204 1074 "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/1e/1e7f53108e1bbfee39ed331ea4b0d7954b8bdf98310ebe2376008835b908bbbe.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/99/99d164fc056b13447a549abffd210bb4338e36ec6fe67d148daa5bb68a9a07a5.png)" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186066382 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186069205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292058 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069205 1071 "![](images/76/768d0be7926a8f66d7b2ac193e842f7567d40e9c2217376b93ed284154520539.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y = k(\\frac{1}{6})^{x} + c$. Find the value of $k - c$." 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186069205 1071 "![](images/76/768d0be7926a8f66d7b2ac193e842f7567d40e9c2217376b93ed284154520539.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186069205 1074 "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=4,$ hence $c=4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,6),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}+4 & =6 \\\\ k+4 & =6 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=2-4=-2.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}+4.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186073819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292618 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186073819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292619 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186073819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292620 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186073819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292621 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186074145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186071037 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186074145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292118 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186074145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186074145 1071 "The distance between the points $A(3, 5,-1)$ and $B(4, 3, 1)$ is {{field-1}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 false] [17592186074145 1071 "The distance between the points $A(3,5,-1)$ and $B(4,3,1)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186074145 1074 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,5,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(4,3,1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(4-3)^{2}+(3-5)^{2}+(1-(-1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186083212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292773 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186083212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292837 13194139533