[17592186292573 1072 #uuid "5490f15b-b3f0-5c47-9bfc-42c68fa91233" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292573 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{x}{10})}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}}{10}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =7-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7)-7}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7)-1} \\\\  & ≈7.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291994 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292574 1069 "q-256499" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292574 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}+8$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292574 1072 #uuid "0e48e9bd-4f64-588a-8bbc-610b3e1586aa" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292574 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-10{x}^{2}+8)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-20x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}+8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}+8)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}-10{x}_{0}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-20{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(0.5)^{4}-10(0.5)^{2}-8}{4(0.5)^{3}-20(0.5)} \\\\  & =1.085\\,526…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}-10{x}_{n}^{2}-8}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-20{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.085\\,526…)^{4}-10(1.085\\,526…)^{2}-8}{4(1.085\\,526…)^{3}-20(1.085\\,526…)} \\\\  & ≈0.941,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291915 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292575 1069 "q-256503" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292575 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.4.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292575 1072 #uuid "665114d9-3edb-5074-a303-9a774aee64e1" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292575 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}-{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{{e}_{n}^{x}-2{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}_{0}^{x}-{x}_{0}^{2}-2}{{e}_{0}^{x}-2{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.4-\\frac{{e}^{1.4}-{1.4}^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.4}-2\\cdot 1.4} \\\\  & =1.4-0.075\\,844… \\\\  & =1.324\\,155….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}_{1}^{x}-{x}_{1}^{2}-2}{{e}_{1}^{x}-2{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.324\\,155…)-\\frac{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-(1.324\\,155…)^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-2\\cdot (1.324\\,155…)} \\\\  & ≈1.319,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291838 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292576 1069 "q-256510" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292576 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2x-1$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292576 1072 #uuid "8c4fa760-3256-5c22-b663-79a18f288418" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292576 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-2x-1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}^{4}-2x-1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-2)-({x}_{n}^{4}-2{x}_{n}-1)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.5)^{4}+1}{4(1.5)^{3}-2} \\\\  & =1.407\\,608…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{4}-1}{4{x}_{1}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.407\\,608…)^{4}-1}{4(1.407\\,608…)^{3}-2} \\\\  & ≈1.396,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291748 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292577 1069 "q-256516" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292577 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292577 1072 #uuid "8c24820e-1dfa-5e5b-808a-a186c977d7a4" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292577 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x & y \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2(1)(1) & 3(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)(1)-1 \\\\ (1)^{3}-(1)^{2}(1)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.5 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292196 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292578 1069 "q-256529" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292578 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.23.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292578 1072 #uuid "48054aa7-f392-5d53-a810-a92b2888173b" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292578 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x)^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.23,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{0}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =1.23-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.23)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.23)-1.23}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.23)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.23)-1} \\\\  & =1.259\\,054…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{1}-{x}_{1}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{1}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{1}-1} \\\\  & =1.259\\,054…-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.259\\,054…)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.259\\,054…)-1.259\\,054…}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1.259\\,054…)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1.259\\,054…)-1} \\\\  & ≈1.259,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292006 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292469 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292501 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1069 "q-256548" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[1,\\,0]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1072 #uuid "45b8a72e-c3af-53f5-abdf-d8718cee05b1" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292579 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3(0) & 2(0)-3(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 3(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3}-3(1)(0)+{0}^{2}-1 \\\\ {1}^{2}+{0}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292168 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292213 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292390 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1069 "q-256549" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[-2,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $-2$ | $-6$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $-1$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1072 #uuid "0af08d6a-ec7d-5f58-bbad-525f07f4e4bc" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292580 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+2(1) & 2(-2)-2(1) \\\\ {1}^{2}-1 & 2(-2)(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+2(-2)(1)-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ (-2)(1)^{2}-(-2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291857 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292062 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292581 1069 "q-256554" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292581 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1$ in the interval $[1.8,2.2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292581 1072 #uuid "0c20466f-73d1-5125-9bdb-69fe3a5c2f0e" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292581 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1)^{′}=2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-{x}_{n}+1}{2{x}_{n}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-{x}_{0}+1}{2{x}_{0}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1.8+1}{2(1.8){sec}^{2}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.066\\,129…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-{x}_{1}+1}{2{x}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =(2.066\\,129…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-(2.066\\,129…)+1}{2(2.066\\,129…){sec}^{2}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.017\\,679….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.066$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.018,$ rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292278 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292313 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292582 1069 "q-256557" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292582 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [-1,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292582 1072 #uuid "89a1a448-da71-581e-a9b4-07c847d8758d" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292582 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(2)(-1)^{3}+1 & 3(2)^{2}(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\ 3(2)^{2} & -4(-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2 \\\\ (2)^{3}-(-1)^{4}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 14 \\\\ 12 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 4-14\\cdot 12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -14 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{180}[\\begin{aligned}-82 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{139}{90} \\\\ -\\frac{53}{60}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.544 \\\\ -0.883\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292231 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292301 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292583 1069 "q-256561" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292583 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292583 1072 #uuid "3e37e908-1ba3-5296-a45b-ce709114e4c1" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292583 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & 3(2)^{2} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}-3(2) & -3(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}+(2)^{3}-6 \\\\ (1)^{3}-3(1)(2)+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-3)-12\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} \\\\ \\frac{53}{30}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.9 \\\\ 1.767\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292376 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292584 1069 "q-256582" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292584 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+4x-6$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.3$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292584 1072 #uuid "013fb993-c34e-58d5-ab5a-96a9dfc2d8f4" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292584 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+4x-6)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+4)-({x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.3)^{3}+6}{3(1.3)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.145…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{1}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.145…)^{3}+6}{3(1.145…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{2}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.134…)^{3}+6}{3(1.134…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.13$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.13$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291872 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291925 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292099 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292172 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1069 "q-256586" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[2,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-12$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-8$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1.9$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1072 #uuid "07553657-3004-575a-8c92-6fc8c4a36408" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292585 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(2)^{3} & -3(2)^{2} \\\\ 2(2)-2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4}-{2}^{3} \\\\ {2}^{2}-(2)(2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}32 & -12 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.9 \\\\ 2.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292517 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292586 1069 "q-256588" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292586 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-1$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292586 1072 #uuid "09d5d54f-fddc-5f68-b1ba-4119ada7542d" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292586 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-1)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+1}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.347….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.347…)^{3}+1}{3(1.347…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.325…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.325…)^{3}+1}{3(1.325…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{2{x}_{3}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{3}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.324…)^{3}+1}{3(1.324…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.32$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.32$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.32,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291986 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292587 1069 "q-256603" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292587 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.25,\\,0.25]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $1.095$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292587 1072 #uuid "e5687681-a311-5b7f-9dca-4cf96185dc21" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292587 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+y-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3(0.25) & 3(0.25)^{2}+3(1.25) \\\\ 3(1.25)^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1.25+(0.25)^{3}+3(1.25)(0.25)-4 \\\\ (1.25)^{3}+0.25-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1.75 & 3.9375 \\\\ 4.6875 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1.796875 \\\\ 0.203125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{1.75\\cdot 1-3.9375\\cdot 4.6875}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3.9375 \\\\ -4.6875 & 1.75\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1.796\\,875 \\\\ 0.203\\,125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{16.707\\,031…}[\\begin{aligned}-2.596\\,679… \\\\ 8.778\\,320…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.095 \\\\ 0.775\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291997 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292588 1069 "q-256604" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292588 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-4$ has a root in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to one decimal place.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292588 1072 #uuid "ce1e65e3-3237-5496-b03f-b34039bbc935" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292588 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-4)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-x-4}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-4)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+4}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.869…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.869…)^{3}+4}{3(1.869…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.799…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+4}{3{x}_{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.799…)^{3}+4}{3(1.799…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.796….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.8$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.8$ are the same when rounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.8,$ rounded to one decimal place." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292504 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292589 1069 "q-256610" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292589 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [0,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-3,\\,3]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $-2.621$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292589 1072 #uuid "56b469e2-15f5-5133-95d9-5154a6948b7f" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292589 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-3)(3)^{3}-1 & 3(-3)^{2}(3)^{2}+4(3)^{3} \\\\ 3(-3)^{2}-2(3) & -2(-3)+2(3)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2}(3)^{3}-(-3)+{3}^{4}-3 \\\\ (-3)^{3}-2(-3)(3)+{3}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-163 & 351 \\\\ 21 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-163)\\cdot 12-351\\cdot 21}[\\begin{aligned}12 & -351 \\\\ -21 & -163\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9327}[\\begin{aligned}3537 \\\\ -6967\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.621 \\\\ 2.253\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292408 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292590 1069 "q-256612" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292590 1071 "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8x-3$ has a root in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=2$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292590 1072 #uuid "79ea7f86-725b-5ac1-990c-02d59dacbbed" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292590 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-8x-3)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-8)-({x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}_{n}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{n}^{3}-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3({x}_{0}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{0}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({2}^{4}+1)}{4({2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{24} \\\\  & =2.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3({x}_{1}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{1}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.125)^{4}+1)}{4((2.125)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.112…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3({x}_{2}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.112…)^{4}+1)}{4((2.112…)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.111….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈2.11$ and ${x}_{3}≈2.11$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈2.11,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291753 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292591 1069 "q-256621" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292591 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[2,5]\\times [0,5]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[5,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[5,\\,5]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2.727$ |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292591 1072 #uuid "8e20d025-cc7a-5777-9e32-357698088f38" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292591 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y-20) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y-20)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(5) & 2(5) \\\\ 2(5) & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(5)^{2}+(5)^{2}-25 \\\\ (5)^{2}-5-20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{10\\cdot (-1)-10\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{110}[\\begin{aligned}-25 \\\\ -250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{105}{22} \\\\ \\frac{30}{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}4.773 \\\\ 2.727\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292135 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292592 1069 "q-256622" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292592 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292592 1072 #uuid "901ae21e-2102-5036-a26a-5ee38f72149b" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292592 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x)^{′}={e}^{x}+3{x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n}}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5)-({e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n})}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+2{x}_{0}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.5){e}^{1.5}+2(1.5)^{3}-{e}^{1.5}}{{e}^{1.5}+3(1.5)^{2}-5} \\\\  & =1.442\\,762….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+2{x}_{1}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}+3{x}_{1}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.442\\,762…){e}^{1.442\\,762…}+2(1.442\\,762…)^{3}-{e}^{1.442\\,762…}}{{e}^{1.442\\,762…}+3(1.442\\,762…)^{2}-5} \\\\  & ≈1.439,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292002 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292593 1069 "q-256623" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292593 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292593 1072 #uuid "31695794-a02a-5139-9ef2-dcc26171634b" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292593 1074 "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=-{e}^{-x}+3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+{x}_{n}^{3}-2{x}_{n}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+{x}_{0}^{3}-2{x}_{0}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{-0.5}+{0.5}^{3}-2\\cdot 0.5}{-{e}^{-0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2} \\\\  & ≈0.355,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292149 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592