{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x-2y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y \\\\ -x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-y \\\\ -x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(0.96) & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3(0.96)^{2}-(-0.48) \\\\ -0.96-2(-0.48)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5.76 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3.2448 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{5.76\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5.76\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3.2448 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.96 \\\\ -0.48\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{12.52}[\\begin{aligned}-6.4896 \\\\ 3.2448\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.442 \\\\ -0.221\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292017 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292043 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292611 1069 "q-257223" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292611 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,32]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+6.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1.5,\\,1.5]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[2.25,\\,2.25]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n| ${x}_{2}=[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292611 1072 #uuid "cbbc25f6-98af-589a-9040-9c5610d1e6b3" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292611 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y-6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y-6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y-6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(2.25)-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(2.25)-6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3(2.25)^{2}-6(2.25) \\\\ 3(2.25)^{2}-6(2.25)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}7.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7.5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1.6875 \\\\ 1.6875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{7.5\\cdot 7.5-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}7.5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1.6875 \\\\ 1.6875\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ 2.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{56.25}[\\begin{aligned}12.656\\,25 \\\\ 12.656\\,25\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.025 \\\\ 2.025\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292365 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292612 1069 "q-257249" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292612 1071 "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,2]\\times [-2,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.75,\\,0.125]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | $-0.301$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292612 1072 #uuid "6a77adf3-74ad-51f8-9ac5-d38adec993b8" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292612 1074 "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-2(0.125)+6 & 4(0.125)-2(1.75) \\\\ 4(0.125)-2(1.75) & 4(1.75)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(0.125)^{2}-2(1.75)(0.125)+6(1.75) \\\\ 4(1.75)(0.125)-(1.75)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5.75 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}10.093\\,75 \\\\ -2.187\\,5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(5.75)\\cdot 7-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5.75\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10.093\\,75 \\\\ -2.187\\,5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{31.25}[\\begin{aligned}64.093\\,75 \\\\ 17.703\\,125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.301 \\\\ -0.442\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291744 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292613 1069 "q-2592" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292613 1071 "Find the integral of $\\frac{z+5}{\\sqrt{z+4}}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292613 1072 #uuid "7de83625-3bca-5002-8a64-075a352f81b8" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292613 1074 "Let $u=z+4.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}z=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$z+5=u+1.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{z+5}{\\sqrt{z+4}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{u+1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{u}{\\sqrt{u}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u+\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u+\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+2\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{(z+4)^{3}}}{3}+2\\sqrt{z+4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292100 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292614 1069 "q-26063" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292614 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292614 1072 #uuid "6007df97-7592-55f0-9719-8190411bf59a" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292614 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(2x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n2x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2\\cdot 2}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292045 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292615 1069 "q-26094" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292615 1071 "Find the solution to the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{5y}{6}-2=0 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292615 1072 #uuid "35a25c61-ba3b-5b21-8d90-ee3bc07b7267" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292615 1074 "To get rid of the fractions in the first equation, we must multiply the first equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $6.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{6}+\\frac{5y}{6}-2=0 & |\\,\\times 6 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+5y-12=0 \\\\ 3x+y+6=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the second equation can easily be solved for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $y=-3x-6$ into the first equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y-12 & =0 \\\\ x+5(-3x-6)-12 & =0 \\\\ x-15x-30-12 & =0 \\\\ -14x-42 & =0 \\\\ -14x & =42 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $y,$ we substitute $x=-3$ into the equation $y=-3x-6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-6 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-6 \\\\  & =9-6 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3,\\,y=3.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292288 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292616 1069 "q-2615" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292616 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292616 1072 #uuid "056e1c78-a9bb-5b9a-a3a2-7b12402a4162" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292616 1074 "Let $u=1-x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$x=1-u.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int (1-u)^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int ({u}^{2}-2u+1)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int ({u}^{5/2}-2{u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-(\\frac{2}{7}{u}^{7/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{u}^{5/2}+\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{7}\\sqrt{(1-x)^{7}}+\\frac{4}{5}\\sqrt{(1-x)^{5}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{(1-x)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292121 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292617 1069 "q-2621" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292617 1071 "Calculate the integral of $(2x+1)\\sqrt{4-x}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292617 1072 #uuid "a9848d15-ae9b-5ed7-b76d-7cc1c522f111" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292617 1074 "Let $u=4-x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$x=4-u.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+1)\\sqrt{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2(4-u)+1)\\sqrt{u}(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\int (9-2u)\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int (9{u}^{1/2}-2{u}^{3/2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-(6{u}^{3/2}-\\frac{4}{5}{u}^{5/2})+C \\\\  & =-6\\sqrt{(4-x)^{3}}+\\frac{4}{5}\\sqrt{(4-x)^{5}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292287 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292618 1069 "q-262213" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292618 1071 "The distance between the points $A(-1,4,-1)$ and $B(2,6,5)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292618 1072 #uuid "ea8b80f5-5852-58eb-a9f5-87a9a29aa672" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292618 1074 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-1,4,-1)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(2,6,5)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1){)}^{2}+(6-4{)}^{2}+(5-(-1){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291972 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292619 1069 "q-262216" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292619 1071 "The distance between the points $A(5,-2,-3)$ and $B(2,2,-3)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292619 1072 #uuid "c45e8a35-554d-5362-9dc0-c76aa841bc0c" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292619 1074 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(5,-2,-3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(2,2,-3)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-5{)}^{2}+(2-(-2){)}^{2}+(-3-(-3){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3{)}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291909 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292620 1069 "q-262221" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292620 1071 "The distance between the points $A(3,1,2)$ and $B(5,-3,4)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292620 1072 #uuid "e16c5fb0-60f5-53f8-bf40-4b5cf72b5d57" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292620 1074 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(3,1,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(5,-3,4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(5-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292102 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292621 1069 "q-262223" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292621 1071 "The distance between the points $A(4,-2,-2)$ and $B(3,-4,2)$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292621 1072 #uuid "36c96529-7715-55dd-9853-b5f7e1d65f9a" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292621 1074 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(4,-2,-2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(3,-4,2)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(3-4{)}^{2}+(-4-(-2){)}^{2}+(2-(-2){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1{)}^{2}+(-2{)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292496 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292622 1069 "q-26245" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292622 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292622 1072 #uuid "832474fd-cb1a-5404-9c37-91cb91e47e2c" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292622 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-1,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292482 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292623 1069 "q-263069" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292623 1071 "If ${e}^{t}=15,$ then $t≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292623 1072 #uuid "b4873899-1d4e-5ac3-af6e-023b6c9c81f9" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292623 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & =15 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{t}) & =ln⁡(15) \\\\ t & =ln⁡(15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nt≈2.708\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292514 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292624 1069 "q-263077" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292624 1071 "If ${e}^{3x}=9,$ then $x≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292624 1072 #uuid "e8fa3225-9eff-5b91-9505-8bb2cf404f2e" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292624 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & =9 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{3x}) & =ln⁡(9) \\\\ 3x & =ln⁡(9) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}ln⁡(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈0.732\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291738 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292625 1069 "q-263081" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292625 1071 "If ${e}^{5-y/2}=\\sqrt[5]{7},$ then $y≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292625 1072 #uuid "a0b41575-560d-5ec9-8c58-ecf5304e673c" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292625 1074 "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5-y/2} & =\\sqrt[5]{7} \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{5-y/2}) & =ln⁡(\\sqrt[5]{7}) \\\\ 5-\\frac{y}{2} & =ln⁡({7}^{1/5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-\\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}ln⁡(7) \\\\ -\\frac{y}{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}ln⁡(7)-5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}ln⁡(7)+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈9.222\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291880 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292022 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292626 1069 "q-263114" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292626 1071 "![](images/d8/d8674de78fc4c8685eb5e76c6beb30e08717ff74d411c71e4c25b784516e3ee8.png)\n\nFind the coordinates $(h,k)$ of the center of the circle above given that the points $(6,-4)$ and $(2,4)$ lie on a diameter of the circle.\n\n$(h,k)=(${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292626 1072 #uuid "d43934eb-0ed6-513c-a145-13003f5f381a" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292626 1074 "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. Therefore, we can calculate it using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6+2}{2},\\frac{-4+4}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{2},\\frac{0}{2}) \\\\  & =(4,0)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291830 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292627 1069 "q-263123" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292627 1071 "The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-6,-4)$ and $(6,1)$, respectively, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle. Determine the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292627 1072 #uuid "757100a6-7faa-5b68-a20a-1dd536cf46e6" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292627 1074 "The diameter can be calculated using the distance formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6-6)^{2}+(-4-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{169} \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is half the diameter:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{d}{2}=\\frac{13}{2}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292404 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292628 1069 "q-263134" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292628 1071 "The points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-3,-4)$ and $(9,8)$, respectively, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle. Determine the radius $r$ of the circle.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292628 1072 #uuid "70bdacec-c94b-5c75-a96b-a685c9810945" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292628 1074 "The diameter can be calculated using the distance formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3-9)^{2}+(-4-8)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-12)^{2}+(-12)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{288} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is half the diameter:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{d}{2}=\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{2}=6\\sqrt{2}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291850 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292059 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292227 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1069 "q-263144" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1071 "The segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is the diameter of a circle where the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $(-7,2)$ and $(5,-6)$, respectively. Find the coordinates of the center and the radius of the circle.\n\nThe circle is centered at  $(${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$)$ and its radius is{{field-3}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1072 #uuid "4739c1ec-a8d3-5934-9d92-06036fc69fc5" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292629 1074 "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-7+5}{2},\\frac{2+(-6)}{2}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2}{2},-\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =(-1,-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(-1,-2)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(5,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-5)}^{2}+{(-2-(-6))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-1,-2),$ and its radius is $2\\sqrt{13}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291825 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291864 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291894 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1069 "q-263155" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1071 "![](images/75/752774e8849a8dfbb2badb1124b4be72f585751d7051eddb57d64c471990ec08.png)\n\nGiven that the points $(4,5)$ and $(2,-3)$ lie on a diameter of a circle, what are the center and radius of the circle?\n\nThe circle is centered at  $(${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$)$ and its radius is{{field-3}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1072 #uuid "f7b78290-811a-51c0-8193-ca733241efb1" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292630 1074 "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4+2}{2},\\frac{5+(-3)}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{6}{2},\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  & =(3,1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(3,1)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(4,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3-4)}^{2}+{(1-5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(3,1),$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{17}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291846 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291853 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292386 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1069 "q-263167" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1071 "![](images/4f/4fe49781cc2874d12d3c53da4785cb6173887f4d39433b0e456efd9208781724.png)\n\nGiven that the points $(10,6)$ and $(-6,8)$ lie on a diameter of a circle, what are the center and radius of the circle?\n\nThe circle is centered at  $(${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$)$ and its radius is{{field-3}}." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1072 #uuid "a49fcea4-6435-5da9-a37e-8ba10be22a22" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292631 1074 "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{10+(-6)}{2},\\frac{6+8}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{2},\\frac{14}{2}) \\\\  & =(2,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(2,7)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(10,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2-10)}^{2}+{(7-6)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(2,7),$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{65}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292120 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292632 1069 "q-263178" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292632 1071 "If ${e}^{x+2}-7=2,$ then $x≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292632 1072 #uuid "1153c8ea-590e-5259-8ecc-5853eab3d5b4" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292632 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2}-7 & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x+2} & =2+7 \\\\ {e}^{x+2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2} & =9 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{x+2}) & =ln⁡(9) \\\\ x+2 & =ln⁡(9) \\\\ x & =ln⁡(9)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈0.197\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292396 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292633 1069 "q-263199" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292633 1071 "If $9-{e}^{3-4x}=5,$ then $x≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292633 1072 #uuid "96c56758-96d0-59b3-b1f1-5393c3950a3d" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292633 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-{e}^{3-4x} & =5 \\\\ -{e}^{3-4x} & =5-9 \\\\ -{e}^{3-4x} & =-4 \\\\ {e}^{3-4x} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3-4x} & =4 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{3-4x}) & =ln⁡(4) \\\\ 3-4x & =ln⁡(4) \\\\ -4x & =ln⁡(4)-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}ln⁡(4)+\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈0.403\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292359 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292634 1069 "q-263201" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292634 1071 "If ${e}^{5+y/2}-9=21,$ then $y≈${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292634 1072 #uuid "dd44a9f8-805f-5834-9354-5593694485e1" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292634 1074 "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5+y/2}-9 & =21 \\\\ {e}^{5+y/2} & =21+9 \\\\ {e}^{5+y/2} & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5+y/2} & =30 \\\\ ln⁡({e}^{5+y/2}) & =ln⁡(30) \\\\ 5+\\frac{y}{2} & =ln⁡(30) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =ln⁡(30)-5 \\\\ y & =2ln⁡(30)-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈-3.198\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292338 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292635 1069 "q-263257" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292635 1071 "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n9,\\,13,\\,2,\\,14,\\,7\n$$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292635 1072 #uuid "e60ca732-7d65-5238-a112-c7e6a9971ee8" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292635 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (9+13+2+14+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 45 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ({9}^{2}+{13}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{14}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (81+169+4+196+49) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 499 \\\\  & =\\frac{499}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{499}{5}-{(9)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{499}{5}-81 \\\\  & =\\frac{499-405}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{94}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292474 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292636 1069 "q-263258" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292636 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of the following data set:\n\n$$\n14,\\,21,\\,13,\\,16\n$$\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292636 1072 #uuid "40581d53-7292-5715-b56f-7c2784c56672" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292636 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=4$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (14+21+13+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(14-16)^{2}+(21-16)^{2}+(13-16)^{2}+(16-16)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4+25+9+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{4} \\\\  & =9.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186292416 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292637 1069 "q-263297" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292637 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of the following data set:\n\n$$\n1,\\,-6,\\,-1,\\,3,\\,-11,\\,-4\n$$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292637 1072 #uuid "8c211885-92fc-5c22-a74f-7184471be943" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292637 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (1-6-1+3-11-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(1-(-3))^{2}+(-6-(-3))^{2}+(-1-(-3))^{2}+(3-(-3))^{2}+(-11-(-3))^{2}+(-4-(-3))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[16+9+4+36+64+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{130}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291924 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292638 1069 "q-263301" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292638 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $8$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=64,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=768.\n$$\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292638 1072 #uuid "c843d879-c182-5566-b5f0-60aef8401119" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292638 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=64,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (64) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2}=768,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 768 \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =96-{(8)}^{2} \\\\  & =96-64 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186291774 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292639 1069 "q-263302" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292639 1071 "Compute the variance ${\\sigma }^{2}$ of a data set ${x}_{i}$ consisting of $7$ data points, where\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=63,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=686.\n$$\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292639 1072 #uuid "b401d75c-3c89-5fbf-b5e9-b004fe9d5890" 13194139533409 17592186045585 true] [17592186292639 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}=63,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (63) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}^{2}=686,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_